   <namePart type=\"given\">Tanmoy</namePart>\nL174:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Chakraborty</namePart>\nL175:                 <role>\nL176:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL177:                 </role>\nL178:             </name>\nL179:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL180:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Carolyn</namePart>\nL181:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Rose</namePart>\nL182:                 <role>\nL183:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL184:                 </role>\nL185:             </name>\nL186:             <name type=\"personal\">\nL187:                 <namePart type=\"given\">Violet</namePart>\nL188:                 <namePart type=\"family\">Peng</namePart>\nL189:                 <role>\nL190:                     <roleTerm authority=\"marcrelator\" type=\"text\">editor</roleTerm>\nL191:                 </role>\nL192:             </name>\nL202:         <abstract>Controllable Dialogue Generation (CDG) enables chatbots to generate responses with desired attributes, and weighted decoding methods have achieved significant success in the CDG task. However, using a fixed constant value to manage the bias of attribute probabilities makes it challenging to find an ideal control strength that satisfies both controllability and fluency.\nL203: To address this issue, we propose ECO decoding Entropy-based COntrol, which dynamically adjusts the control strength at each generation step according to the model’s entropy in both the language model and attribute classifier probability distributions. Experimental results on DailyDialog and MultiWOZ datasets show that our method achieves improved control accuracy while maintaining fluency and grammar, outperforming previous decoding methods across various models and settings.\nL204: Furthermore, ECO decoding alleviates probability interpolation issues in multi-attribute generation, demonstrating its robust performance in both single and multi-attribute scenarios.</abstract>\nL205:         <identifier type=\"citekey\">shin-etal-2025-eco</identifier>\nL206:         <identifier type=\"doi\">10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.1437</identifier>\nL207:         <location>\nL208:             <url>https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.1437/</url>\nL209:         </location>\nL210:         <part>\nL211:             <date>2025-11</date>\nL212:             <extent unit=\"page\">\nL213:                 <start>28309</start>\nL214:                 <end>28321</end>\nL215:             </extent>\nL216:         </part>\nL217:     </mods>\nL218:     </modsCollection>\nL219: Download as File\nL220: \nL221:     %0 Conference Proceedings\nL222:     %T ECO Decoding: Entropy-Based Control for Controllability and Fluency in Controllable Dialogue Generation\nL223:     %A Shin, Seungmin\nL224:     %A Kim, Dooyoung\nL225:     %A Ko, Youngjoong\nL226:     %Y Christodoulopoulos, Christos\nL227:     %Y Chakraborty, Tanmoy\nL228:     %Y Rose, Carolyn\nL229:     %Y Peng, Violet\nL230:     %S Proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing\nL231:     %D 2025\nL232:     %8 November\nL233:     %I Association for Computational Linguistics\nL234:     %C Suzhou, China\nL235:     %@ 979-8-89176-332-6\nL236:     %F shin-etal-2025-eco\nL237:     %X Controllable Dialogue Generation (CDG) enables chatbots to generate responses with desired attributes, and weighted decoding methods have achieved significant success in the CDG task. However, using a fixed constant value to manage the bias of attribute probabilities makes it challenging to find an ideal control strength that satisfies both controllability and fluency.\nL238: To address this issue, we propose ECO decoding Entropy-based COntrol, which dynamically adjusts the control strength at each generation step according to the model’s entropy in both the language model and attribute classifier probability distributions. Experimental results on DailyDialog and MultiWOZ datasets show that our method achieves improved control accuracy while maintaining fluency and grammar, outperforming previous decoding methods across various models and settings.\nL239: Furthermore, ECO decoding alleviates probability interpolation issues in multi-attribute generation, demonstrating its robust performance in both single and multi-attribute scenarios.\nL240:     %R 10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.1437\nL241:     %U https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.1437/\nL242:     %U https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.emnlp-main.1437\nL243:     %P 28309-28321\nL244: Download as File\nL245: ##### Markdown (Informal)\nL246: \nL247: [ECO Decoding: Entropy-Based Control for Controllability and Fluency in Controllable Dialogue Generation](https://aclanthology.org/2025.emnlp-main.1437/) (Shin et al., EMNLP 2025)\nL248: \nL249:   * cite34†ECO Decoding: Entropy-Based Control for Controllability and Fluency in Controllable Dialogue Generation (Shin et al., EMNLP 2025)\nL250: ##### ACL\nL251: \nL252:   * Seungmin Shin, Dooyoung Kim, and Youngjoong Ko. 2025. cite34†ECO Decoding: Entropy-Based Control for Controllability and Fluency in Controllable Dialogue Generation . In Proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing, pages 28309–28321, Suzhou, China. Association for Computational Linguistics.\nL253: ACL materials are Copyright © 1963–2026 ACL; other materials are copyrighted by their respective copyright holders. Materials prior to 2016 here are licensed under the cite37†Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 3.0 International License†creativecommons.org . Permission is granted to make copies for the purposes of teaching and research. Materials published in or after 2016 are licensed on a cite38†Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License†creativecommons.org .\nL254: The ACL Anthology is managed and built by the cite5